  "privateAlbumHideNoBucketTitle": "This phone will be the only copy",
  "privateAlbumHideAnyway": "Hide Anyway",
  "privateAlbumBackupNoBucket": "Hidden photos are kept encrypted on this phone, so the album works offline. But hiding takes the photo out of Photos, and with no bucket set up there is no second copy anywhere — delete the app and it goes with it.",
  "privateAlbumBackupRecentlyDeleted": "Photos keeps deleted items for 30 days. Hiding isn't complete until you empty Recently Deleted in Photos.",
  "privateAlbumHowTitle": "HOW THIS WORKS",
  "privateAlbumHowBullets": "Every code opens an album. A code that hasn't been used opens an empty one, so nothing on this phone reveals whether anything is hidden.\nHiding removes the photo from Photos. On this phone it is kept in a folder left out of the iPhone backup, named after nothing.\nWhat's uploaded to your bucket looks like an ordinary photo: a different picture from your library, with your hidden photo encrypted inside it.\nThe decoy is chosen to be about the same file size as your photo, so it doesn't stand out.\nThe decoy never carries your photo's date, and nothing records which decoy holds which photo.\nYour passphrase and 4-digit code together form the key. Neither is stored. The key the passphrase makes, and the keys of albums you have opened, are kept in this phone's Keychain so backups carry on without asking.\nThe database records which photos are hidden under a keyed tag of the code, not the code. The app-data backups leave hidden photos out.\nA photo sent back to the bucket keeps its tile here; thumbnails come down 64 KB at a time.\nHiding deletes the photo's plain copy from your bucket, but only once its encrypted copy is in every bucket. Un-hiding does the reverse.\nOn a new phone, your passphrase and code bring the hidden photos back from the bucket. Hidden photos that never reached a bucket are only on this phone.\nAnyone who reads this app's source code can identify the disguised photos and count them, but still cannot open one.",
